<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gijón</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xixón</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gijón is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> with Roman origins. In the eighth century it was a seat of Muslim authority whose governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munuza">Munuza</a> faced the revolt of Pelagius. It grew into an industrial and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-exporting port, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> it was the seat of the Sovereign Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturias</a> and León until Franco's forces took it on 21 October 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gijon.jpg" alt="Gijón" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Veriña · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xix%C3%B3n_dende_l%27aire_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
